//! check: fail(E1001)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: std.net, mem.tier0.move.1, mem.tier0.move.2
//!
//! **The staleness discipline, held as a rejection** — the `std.fs.File`
//! precedent applied to a socket. `net.close` takes its `Socket` by `take`
//! (X1), so writing to a closed socket is a use-after-move: caught
//! statically, with no generation counter, no liveness bit and no run-time
//! cost. This file is expected to be REJECTED, and if a future pin ever
//! accepts it the rig goes red, which is exactly what a discipline that
//! lives in the type system should do.
//!
//! It is also the argument for `take` over a `mut`-and-flag design: a
//! `Socket` with an `is_open` field would move this error from compile time
//! to run time and add a row to every operation for a condition the
//! compiler can see. The forged-handle row (`forged_handle_io_row.lu`) is
//! what remains for the descriptors the compiler cannot see.

use std.net

fn main() -> !int {
    var srv = net.listen(net.loopback(0))?
    let p = net.port(srv)?
    var cli = net.connect(net.loopback(p))?
    net.close(take cli)?
    net.write(mut cli, "after the close")?
    net.close_listener(take srv)?
    0
}
